%S A399306 2,5,6,9,13,16,17,20,23,24,27,31,34,35,38,42,45,46,49,52,53,56,60,63,
%T A399306 64,67,70,71,74,78,81,82,85,89,92,93,96,99,100,103,107,110,111,114,
%U A399306 118,121,122,125,128,129,132,136,139,140,143,146,147,150,154,157,158,161,165
%N A399306 Integers whose minimal Lucas representation (A130310) does not end in a 0 which if removed results in a valid minimal Lucas representation of another integer.
%C A399306 These might be called the "Lucas-odd" numbers.
%C A399306 Integers whose minimal Lucas representation ends either in 1 or in 1010. A minimal Lucas representation may not end in 101, so the final 0 of representations ending in 1010 cannot be removed.
%C A399306 The terms of A054770 comprise a subset of the terms of this sequence.
%C A399306 The similarity of this sequence with A094350 is striking, but the terms 41 and 117 (=76+41) occur in A094350 but not here. It is possible that the terms of this sequence comprise a subset of the terms of A094350, but this is merely a conjecture.
%H A399306 John L. Brown, Jr., <a href="https://www.fq.math.ca/Scanned/7-3/brown.pdf">Unique representations of integers as sums of distinct Lucas numbers</a>, The Fibonacci Quarterly, Vol. 7, No. 3 (1969), pp. 243-252.
%H A399306 Hung V. Chu, David C. Luo and Steven J. Miller, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2004.08316">On Zeckendorf Related Partitions Using the Lucas Sequence</a>, arXiv:2004.08316 [math.NT], 2020-2021.
%Y A399306 Cf. A130310, A000032, A342089, A054770. Complement of A399305.
